# A rack and pinion drive: a falling weight turning a flywheel.
#
#   mount --HINGE-- pinion        the flywheel, on its bearing
#   guide --PRISMATIC-- bar       the rack, on its slide
#   pinion =RACK= bar             the teeth
#
#   5 + 5 + 1 = 11 rows on 12 freedoms, leaving one — the drive.
#
# Every joint in this scene was added for it. The HINGE was always here;
# the RACK ties the pinion's turn to the bar's travel, Ds = r*theta; and
# the PRISMATIC is the guide, which is what a rack actually runs in.
# Drop the guide and the reaction torque twists the bar 24.3 degrees off
# square and shoves it 0.68 off its line. With it, both are ZERO — the
# twist below reads 2.6e-22 degrees and the sideways stray is exactly
# 0.0, not merely small.
#
# THE CLOSED FORM. The rack has nothing holding it up but the pinion, so
# it falls — and the pinion's inertia resists through the teeth. One
# degree of freedom, so one equation:
#
#     a = m g / (m + I/r^2)
#
# The flywheel does not act with its mass but with I/r^2, its inertia
# referred through the pitch radius. For a solid disc I = M r^2 / 2, so
#
#     I/r^2 = M/2       — independent of the radius entirely
#
# and with a pinion twice the rack's mass that term is exactly m. The
# rack therefore falls at
#
#     a = g/2      exactly, whatever the pitch radius
#
# which is the number to watch: the weight falls at half gravity because
# the flywheel it is winding up is, referred through the teeth, exactly
# as heavy as itself.
#
# Measured here, against -a t^2 / 2 over four seconds and a 1.6 drop:
#
#     200 cold restarts, one per frame        5.35e-4
#     the same four seconds run continuously  3.74e-5
#
# a factor of 14, and the same restart-versus-continuation effect as
# every other recording (grammar §12.9).
set system.g_constant = 0
set system.uniform_gravity = [0, -0.4, 0]
collide off
# The bearing: a POINT at the pinion's own centre, so the midpoint pivot
# lands there and the flywheel turns about its axle.
new point as mount { mass = 1, position = [0, 0, 0], inverse_mass = 0 }
new cylinder as pinion { mass = 2, radius = 0.6, half_height = 0.05, position = [0, 0, 0] }
# The guide fixes the line the rack slides on. A PRISMATIC holds no
# pivot — it holds the offset ACROSS the axis at whatever it is when the
# joint is made — so the guide sits where the bar starts.
new point as guide { mass = 1, position = [0.6, 0, 0], inverse_mass = 0 }
new cuboid as bar { mass = 1, half_extents = [0.06, 1.2, 0.06], position = [0.6, 0, 0] }
hinge mount pinion [0, 0, 1]
prismatic guide bar [0, 1, 0]
# Pitch radius 0.6: the bar travels 0.6 per radian of pinion, and the
# bar's line stands exactly that far from the axle, which is what makes
# the picture a rack meshing with a pinion rather than two loose parts.
# The radius was 0.4 in an earlier cut of this scene and the fall is
# unchanged, which is the radius-independence above, demonstrated rather
# than asserted.
rack pinion bar [0, 0, 1] [0, 1, 0] 0.6
method ida
